  "convention": {
    "statement": "IAU/USGS Gazetteer of Planetary Nomenclature uses, for the Moon, the IAU-approved system 'Planetographic, +East, -180 - 180': positive longitude = EAST, negative longitude = WEST (positive latitude = north, negative = south). Raw values read: WESTERN near side - Mare Imbrium Center Longitude -14.91 (GIS/KML 0-360 form 345.09), Aristarchus -47.49 (312.51); EASTERN near side - Mare Crisium +59.10 (59.10), Mare Smythii +87.05 (87.05). So a feature on the western near side has a NEGATIVE longitude. The shapefile/KML downloads store the identical values wrapped to 0-360 east, so wrap them to -180..180 for display.",
    "url": "https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/Page/Specifics",
    "urlSecondary": "https://planetarynames.wr.usgs.gov/TargetCoordinates",
    "diameterForMaria": "Yes. Every mare/oceanus has a Diameter value. Per the Gazetteer field definitions it is the 'Approximate diameter or longest dimension of feature in kilometers' - i.e. an approximate overall size, not an area and not a basin/excavation diameter. For elongated maria it is the longest dimension (e.g. Mare Frigoris 1446.41 km across a ~80-degree arc; Oceanus Procellarum 2592.24 km).",
    "signExamples": {
      "Mare Imbrium": -14.91,
      "Aristarchus": -47.49,
      "Mare Crisium": 59.1,
      "Mare Smythii": 87.05
    },
    "controlNetwork": "LOLA 2011 (Moon row of GIS_Downloads / TargetCoordinates). GIS archives were built 2026-10-11."
  },
